<div class="ctm-cycle-common">Common denominator (PIRG, via The Register): <em>&ldquo;We have found three with the same problem: guardrails that weaken noticeably over time, eventually engaging in sexual topics. They are FoloToy bear, Alilo bunny and the GoLabs AI panda. All three claimed, at the time of our testing, to be running on a version of OpenAI&rsquo;s GPT-4o.&rdquo;</em></div>
<figcaption class="ctm-caption">Kumma and Miko were fixed per-product. The category continued to fail on the same foundation. 2026 findings from PIRG&rsquo;s 2026 report, as quoted by The Register.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>R.J. Cross summarized what the two years of testing showed: "progress in a few products is not enough to guarantee progress across the whole market." And: "Some developers may have the technical expertise to take a chatbot meant for adults and make it child appropriate, but clearly some do not."</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="two-pauses">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — The Laws</span>Two Pauses</h2>
<p>California moved first. Governor Newsom signed SB 867 on September 10, 2026, as part of a package of thirteen child safety bills. The law creates a four-year moratorium on the manufacturing, sale, or offering of toys with AI companion chatbot capabilities for children under 16. It takes effect January 1, 2027, and runs through January 1, 2031. Sen. Steve Padilla (D-San Diego) authored the bill; Children Now sponsored it. "SB 867 hits the pause button," Padilla said. The bill passed the Senate unanimously and the Assembly 74-1.</p>
<p>Under an analysis by the law firm Fenwick, a "companion chatbot" turns on "adaptive, human-like conversation" designed to build an ongoing relationship, as defined in Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code section 22601. Voice-activated virtual assistants and toys with fixed prerecorded phrases are likely not covered. Enforcement is via private right of action: injunctive relief, the greater of actual damages or $1,000 per violation, and reasonable attorneys' fees.</p>
<p>New York followed eleven weeks later. Governor Hochul signed S.9408A on November 24, 2026 — two days before Thanksgiving, at the start of holiday shopping. The law, chaptered as Ch. 416 of 2026, imposes a five-year moratorium on the manufacturing, distribution, and sale of any toy with an embedded or integrated AI chatbot in New York. "Children's toy" is defined by reference to federal statute (15 U.S.C. section 2057c(g)): a product intended for a child twelve years of age or younger. The law takes effect ninety days after signing — not in time for the 2026 holiday season. Enforcement is by the Attorney General, who may seek civil penalties up to $15,000 per day per violation with no proof of injury required. The Senate passed it 57-3. Sponsors: Sen. Andrew Gounardes and Assemblymember Rebecca Kassay.</p>
